(# The statements on their own: the problem set without the answers anywhere in it.

   One problem per page, the way `solutions.jtex` next door sets one solution per page -- the two
   documents are the same shape for the two halves of the booklet, and that is the whole point of
   having this one.

   What it adds that nothing else had: the statements as their own PDF. Until now they existed
   only inside `booklet.pdf`, which carries the solutions and the answers with them, so there was
   no document to hand to a proofreader -- or to a competitor -- without the whole key in it.
   `tearoff.pdf` and `online.pdf` do paginate the statements, but neither is this: a tearoff is
   cut into slips and the online one is burst into a PDF per problem. #)

(@ extends 'base-booklet.jtex' @)

(@ set target = 'booklet' @)

(@ block content @)
    \setcounter{volume}{(* volume.number *)}%
    \pagestyle{naboj}

    (@ if language.booklet.contents.problems @)
        \section{(* i18n[language.id].section.problems *)}
        \pagestyle{problems}
        (@ for problem in volume.problems @)%
            (@ include 'blocks/problem.jtex' @)%
            \newpage
        (@ endfor @)
    (@ endif @)
(@ endblock @)
